    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An Early Postclassic state ruled from Tula in Hidalgo, a city of perhaps 30,000–60,000 people at its height in the 11th century.</li><li>Its warrior statues, colonnaded halls and chacmools have close parallels at Chichén Itzá, though the nature of that link is debated.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> revered the Toltecs as founders of civilisation; Tula itself was abandoned around 1150, amid a severe drought.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> the word toltecatl, ‘Toltec’, came to mean a skilled artisan, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> called art and refinement toltecayotl, ‘Toltec-ness’.</p></aside>
    </section>

