    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl.jpg" alt="Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl">Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quetzalcoatl_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">author unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Toltec state took shape at Tula, in the valley of the Tula River in the present Mexican state of Hidalgo, just north of the Basin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. While the region was still under the control of Teotihuacan, settlers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coyotlatelco">Coyotlatelco</a> tradition, whose pottery links them to the Basin of Mexico, founded hilltop villages in the valley, and from about 600–650 one community began to build a monumental civic centre, known today as Tula Chico. Around 900, in the transition that archaeologists call the Terminal Corral phase, construction began on a larger centre some 1.5 kilometres to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site">Tula Grande</a>. During the following Tollan phase (c. 900–1150) the city drew in the populations of Tula Chico and of neighbouring centres such as Cerro Magoni, which had probably been separate polities. Colonial chronicles say that the Toltecs combined Tolteca-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a> migrants from the north with the Nonoalca, a people associated with the Gulf Coast, and they credit the city’s rise to leaders such as Mixcoatl and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl">Ce Acatl Topiltzin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>, figures of legend rather than of history. The city’s full name, Tollan-Xicocotitlan, used the title Tollan, usually translated ‘place of reeds’, which was given to great cities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site.jpg" alt="Tula (Mesoamerican site)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site">Tula (Mesoamerican site)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TulaSite81.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AlejandroLinaresGarcia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At its height in the eleventh and early twelfth centuries Tula covered some 16 square kilometres, and estimates of its population range from about 30,000 to 60,000, with perhaps 20,000 to 25,000 more in the surrounding countryside. Excavated neighbourhoods show families living in groups of houses built around shared courtyards. Farming villages multiplied across the Tula region during the Tollan phase, and the city drew food and other revenue from this hinterland. Farmers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash and probably cultivated maguey on the dry hillsides for fibre and for the fermented drink pulque. Craft production was central to the economy. Workshops turned obsidian, including the prized green obsidian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-de-las-navajas">Sierra de las Navajas</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a>, into blades and tools, and Tula became a major centre of the obsidian trade. Long-distance exchange brought Plumbate pottery from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and polychrome vessels from Costa Rica. Religion was public and spectacular: the art of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site">Tula Grande</a> honours the feathered serpent and the morning star, and reclining stone figures known as chacmools probably received offerings. Two large ballcourts stood beside the main plaza, a sign of the importance of the ballgame in civic and religious life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Warfare dominates Toltec art. On the summit of Pyramid B stand the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantes-of-tula">Atlantes of Tula</a>, four basalt pillars about 4.6 metres tall carved as warriors with butterfly-shaped breastplates, disc-shaped ornaments on their backs and spear-throwers and darts in their hands. Carved panels on the pyramid show prowling jaguars and coyotes and eagles devouring hearts, images often linked to warrior orders like the eagle and jaguar warriors of later Aztec society. Beside the pyramid ran the Coatepantli, a ‘serpent wall’ with reliefs of snakes devouring skeletons, and the benches of nearby colonnaded halls were carved with processions of armed and richly dressed men. A low platform beside the second ballcourt is usually identified as a skull rack, although some archaeologists attribute it to the later Aztec reoccupation of the site. No battle fought by the Toltec state is securely recorded, however, and the reach of its conquests is debated. Its direct rule probably covered the Tula region and parts of the Basin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and nearby valleys. Michael E. Smith and Lisa Montiel concluded in 2001 that Tula was more a kingdom than an empire, and Dan Healan and Robert Cobean have found no evidence that it ruled one.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl.jpg" alt="Quetzalcoatl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quetzalc%C3%B3atl_como_la_serpiente_emplumada_y_el_dios_del_viento_Eh%C3%A9catl,_en_el_folio_19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tula left no readable written records, so its government is known only in outline. The scale of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site">Tula Grande</a>, with its great plaza, pyramids, ballcourts and halls, shows rulers able to command large workforces, and the Palacio Quemado (Burnt Palace), a complex of colonnaded halls lined with carved benches beside Pyramid B, was probably a setting for the councils, assemblies and ceremonies of the ruling elite. Some archaeologists, including Healan and Cobean, think that power may have been shared among different sectors of society, as it may have been at Teotihuacan, rather than concentrated in a single dynasty. The colonial chronicles, by contrast, describe Tollan as a kingdom and list its kings, but the lists disagree with one another, and in some of them reigns last a round 52 years, the length of a Mesoamerican calendar cycle, so they are treated as legend. The two most famous figures are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl">Ce Acatl Topiltzin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>, a priest-king driven into exile by the trickery of the god Tezcatlipoca, and Huemac, the last ruler. Historians have read them in different ways: Wigberto Jiménez Moreno made Ce Acatl one of Tula’s first rulers and Huemac its last, while Paul Kirchhoff argued that the two were contemporaries who both witnessed its fall.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/popol-vuh.jpg" alt="Popol Vuh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Popol_vuh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco Ximénez, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tula’s art and architecture have close parallels elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. The Temple of the Warriors at Chichén Itzá in Yucatán closely resembles Pyramid B, though on a larger scale, and the two cities share colonnaded halls, chacmools and warrior imagery. Once explained as a Toltec conquest of Yucatán, the connection is now usually seen as contact between two powerful capitals, with influence that may have run in both directions. Tula’s deeper legacy lay in memory. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, Tollan was the model of civilisation: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> a toltecatl, ‘Toltec’, came to mean a skilled artisan, and toltecayotl, ‘Toltec-ness’, meant art and refinement, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> informants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun">Bernardino de Sahagún</a> credited the Toltecs with inventing medicine and the calendar. Ruling houses claimed Toltec descent: the lords of Culhuacan did so, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> chose as their first king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acamapichtli">Acamapichtli</a>, who had Culhua ancestry. Far to the south, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a> of the K’iche’ Maya traces the ancestors of their lords to a place called Tulan. Modern excavation began with Désiré Charnay in the 1880s, and from 1940 Jorge R. Acosta excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site">Tula Grande</a> and restored Pyramid B, setting up again on its summit the Atlantes that had been found broken in a trench beside it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tula’s end came in the mid-twelfth century. Recent archaeomagnetic dating places the close of the Tollan phase at about 1150, when a severe drought set in that lasted from 1149 to about 1167 and would have hit farming hard in this semi-arid region. The city was not destroyed in a single blow. Its abandonment seems to have been gradual, and the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site">Tula Grande</a>, including the Palacio Quemado around 1175, followed abandonment closely in time; Tula Chico, too, was burned during or after the Tollan phase. Dan Healan, Robert Cobean and their colleagues have rejected the older view that invaders bringing Aztec II pottery sacked the city, since Tula Grande was already in ruins when such groups settled there. The chronicles tell instead of strife and calamity, of the flight of Huemac, who in several accounts died at Chapultepec, and of Toltec groups dispersing to Culhuacan, Cholula and other centres. The ruins remained a place of memory. Aztec-period settlers reoccupied parts of the old city, placed burials and offerings among its buildings and removed sculptures, including a chacmool later found in the sacred precinct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, while Toltec prestige passed to the city-states of the Basin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Toltec state, ruled from Tula between about 900 and 1150, was the leading power of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in the Early Postclassic; its warrior art, trade and parallels with Chichén Itzá show its reach, and its memory made ‘Toltec’ a byword for civilisation and a source of royal legitimacy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> and others.</em></p>

